Session 333 summary — the RULE-5 diff review of the 0.12.2 D-513 landing is complete, and it **stop-shipped the build**: 6 required fixes before any rig cycle (the sess325 precedent repeated — a post-landing review catching real holes).

What this session established:

1. **Three of my own flagged concerns confirmed as stop-ships**: (A) the barrier's fswide `-EIO` abort paths skip the late-death defer and never set the aborting slot's `terminal` bit — needs a common abort exit and a classify API change; (B) the classifier's legacy-backfill arm imports a possibly-raced outcome record without the validation the direct-read arm applies — needs one shared validating import helper across all three import sites; (D) the **worst one** — the mount barrier still passes a NULL verdict to the foreign-slice replay, so a lone survivor cold-starting against a torn slice loops `-EBUSY` restarts forever without ever publishing the refusal or reaching the operator-facing quarantine message. The target defect survives on the mount path; the reap path's publish/conflict state machine must be factored into a shared helper the barrier also runs.

2. **Three additional blockers from the reviewer**: the backfill needs a `victim_slot` identity check (I verified the descriptor struct has no reason/scope fields, so that part of the ask is unimplementable and documented as such); backfill `-EPROTO` must fail closed instead of retrying forever; and the barrier needs an unconditional FSWIDE gate at start and at the admission boundary. Items C, E, F were ruled acceptable as landed.

3. Everything captured durably: full work order with code geography in ccmemory `ccloop-c7ee71c6-sess333-GPT-ruling-d513-0.12.2-review-6-stopships`, handoff.md rewritten to point at it. No code was edited — the tree is exactly the reviewed 0.12.2 sv `0C47E91907CFA130AFB6520`.

